            "text": "{\"findings\":[{\"tipo\":\"GC\",\"causa_raiz\":\"Alocação excessiva de memória no método StressTestScene.Update(), gerando aproximadamente 10.28 MB de GC Alloc por frame. Isso provoca pausas catastróficas de Garbage Collection (16.05ms apenas em coleta de lixo), estourando o limite de 16.7ms do alvo de 60 FPS.\",\"codigo_refatorado\":\"using UnityEngine;\\\\n\\\\nnamespace PerformanceAdvisor.StressTest\\\\n{\\\\n    public class StressTestScene : MonoBehaviour\\\\n    {\\\\n        [SerializeField] private int _elementCount = 10000;\\\\n        private float[] _reusableBuffer;\\\\n        private float _timer;\\\\n\\\\n        private void Awake()\\\\n        {\\\\n            _reusableBuffer = new float[_elementCount];\\\\n        }\\\\n\\\\n        private void Update()\\\\n        {\\\\n            ProcessDataNonAlloc(_reusableBuffer);\\\\n        }\\\\n\\\\n        private void ProcessDataNonAlloc(float[] buffer)\\\\n        {\\\\n            float dt = Time.deltaTime;\\\\n            for (int i = 0; i \u003c buffer.Length; i++)\\\\n            {\\\\n                buffer[i] = Mathf.Sin(i + dt);\\\\n            }\\\\n        }\\\\n    }\\\\n}\",\"explicacao_didatica\":\"Toda vez que você cria listas, arrays ou objetos com a palavra-chave 'new' dentro do Update, a Unity cria esses dados na memória temporária (Heap). Quando a memória enche, a Unity precisa congelar o jogo temporariamente (Garbage Collector) para limpar a sujeira acumulada. Para resolver isso, criamos as listas e arrays apenas uma vez no início (Awake ou Start) e apenas limpamos e reutilizamos essas mesmas variáveis em cada frame, eliminando o lixo e as travadinhas.\"},{\"tipo\":\"CPU\",\"causa_raiz\":\"Carga computacional de 55.60ms na thread principal dentro de StressTestScene.Update(), somando um tempo total de CPU de 240.29ms por frame. Executar cálculos pesados em laços sequenciais síncronos na thread principal inviabiliza atingir 60 FPS.\",\"codigo_refatorado\":\"using UnityEngine;\\\\nusing Unity.Collections;\\\\nusing Unity.Jobs;\\\\nusing Unity.Mathematics;\\\\n\\\\nnamespace PerformanceAdvisor.StressTest\\\\n{\\\\n    public class StressTestSceneOptimized : MonoBehaviour\\\\n    {\\\\n        [SerializeField] private int _elementCount = 100000;\\\\n        private NativeArray\u003cfloat\u003e _dataArray;\\\\n        private JobHandle _jobHandle;\\\\n\\\\n        private struct ComputeJob : IJobParallelFor\\\\n        {\\\\n            public float TimeOffset;\\\\n            public NativeArray\u003cfloat\u003e Results;\\\\n\\\\n            public void Execute(int index)\\\\n            {\\\\n                Results[index] = math.sin(index + TimeOffset);\\\\n            }\\\\n        }\\\\n\\\\n        private void Awake()\\\\n        {\\\\n            _dataArray = new NativeArray\u003cfloat\u003e(_elementCount, Allocator.Persistent);\\\\n        }\\\\n\\\\n        private void Update()\\\\n        {\\\\n            _jobHandle.Complete();\\\\n\\\\n            var job = new ComputeJob\\\\n            {\\\\n                TimeOffset = Time.time,\\\\n                Results = _dataArray\\\\n            };\\\\n\\\\n            _jobHandle = job.Schedule(_dataArray.Length, 64);\\\\n        }\\\\n\\\\n        private void LateUpdate()\\\\n        {\\\\n            _jobHandle.Complete();\\\\n        }\\\\n\\\\n        private void OnDestroy()\\\\n        {\\\\n            _jobHandle.Complete();\\\\n            if (_dataArray.IsCreated)\\\\n            {\\\\n                _dataArray.Dispose();\\\\n            }\\\\n        }\\\\n    }\\\\n}\",\"explicacao_didatica\":\"O processador do seu computador tem vários núcleos (como vários trabalhadores), mas o Update tradicional roda em apenas um deles. Se colocamos muito trabalho no Update, esse único trabalhador fica sobrecarregado e a tela engasga. Usando o C# Job System, dividimos a tarefa pesada entre todos os núcleos do processador ao mesmo tempo, fazendo o trabalho rodar muito mais rápido sem travar a taxa de quadros.\"}],\"dicas_level_design\":null}",
